# Drafted by Gemini (R11 batch b3), edited. old_rusty's first conversation.

~ start
set old_rusty_met
Narrator: Birch twigs scrape across cold cobbles in a slow, steady rhythm. Old Rusty peers out from a tangle of red-grey hair, smelling of damp leaves and hearth smoke.
old_rusty [neutral]: Step on a crack, break the moon's back. Wipe your boots before you tread the Round, strangers.
-> menu

~ menu
old_rusty [weary]: Leaves fall down, faces look down. What do you want with an old broom?
* Ask what he does here -> work
* Ask about the oak and the square -> village
* Ask what he mutters about at night -> rumour
* [Insight DC 14] Watch his eyes when he looks toward the hill -> secret_ok | secret_fail
* Leave him to his sweeping -> END

~ work
old_rusty [sly]: Sweep the stone, keep the bone. The village feeds the belly, the broom feeds the square, and the dirt never sleeps.
-> menu

~ village
old_rusty [neutral]: The stone man has no face to lie with. The old oak has too many, and every one of them is thirsty when the axe comes down.
-> menu

~ rumour
old_rusty [afraid]: Six chimes from the tower, one for each hour. When the antlers grow on the little brown hare, lock your shutter and say your prayer.
-> menu

~ secret_ok
Narrator: His knuckles whiten on the ash handle whenever his gaze drifts toward the crooked gable high on the ridge. An old white scar splits his scalp beneath the wild hair.
old_rusty [afraid]: Crooked beam, crooked stair, nobody left to sit in the chair. Don't go up the hill, don't knock on the door.
-> menu

~ secret_fail
old_rusty [neutral]: Wind in the gorse, nothing worse. Mind where you put your heels, I just swept there.
-> menu
